## Hard rules

### R1 — Use the chosen UI library. Do not hand-roll.

When you build a new page, restore a page from a reference image (Figma / screenshot / `.pen` design), or extend an existing screen:

- **First** check whether `%UI_LIBRARY%` already provides the needed primitive (button / input / dialog / popover / sheet / dropdown / tabs / table / card / form / toast / tooltip / select / combobox / accordion / avatar / badge / pagination / skeleton / progress / switch / checkbox / radio / slider / date-picker / drawer / command palette / menubar / breadcrumb / alert / separator).
- **Reach for that component**, not a `<div>` + Tailwind that imitates it.
- If the component is not installed yet, install it first:
  %UI_INSTALL_HINT%
- Compose; only style with %ATOMIC_CSS% utilities for layout (spacing, grid, flex, responsive) — never to re-create chrome that the library renders for you.
- Icons come from `%ICONS_LIB%`. Do not inline SVG or pull a second icon package.

Re-implementing a button / input / dialog by hand is a regression even if it looks visually identical. The library wins on accessibility, focus management, keyboard handling, theming, and cross-stack consistency.

### R2 — Validate at the boundary

Every external input (HTTP response, URL params, form input, IPC payload, file contents, localStorage read, env var) goes through a Zod schema (`*.schema.ts`) at the edge. Internal code consumes the inferred type, not `unknown`.

### R3 — Four async UI states, always

Any view that triggers an async call must render **loading**, **empty**, **error**, **success**. A bare success path is incomplete and will be rejected at review. See `%REPO_RAW_BASE%boundaries/common/async-states.md`.

### R4 — Feature-first folders, shared/ for genuine primitives

```
src/
  app/            # bootstrap, providers, router
  features/<name>/
    schemas/      *.schema.ts
    api/          *.api.ts
    %DATA_DIR%/   %DATA_HOOK_PATTERN%
    components/   *.tsx | *.vue
    %VIEWS_DIR%/  page/screen-level orchestration
  components/     # cross-feature UI (rarely added)
  shared/         # http, lib, styles, native — primitives only
  stores/         # cross-feature state stores (only if needed)
```

Move code into `shared/` **only** after it has been used by 2+ features. Premature shared utilities are forbidden.

### R5 — Centralized HTTP

All network access goes through `src/shared/http/client.ts` (`http.get/post/put/del`). The client already handles base URL, JSON parse, error mapping to `AppError`, and `AbortSignal` propagation. **Do not** call `fetch` directly in feature code.

### R6 — Typed contracts at component boundaries

Components expose typed `props` and emit named events/callbacks. They never read global state they were not given (no `useStore()` deep inside a presentational `<Card>`). Logic that crosses two components moves to a composable / hook / service.

### R7 — Tokens, not literals

Colors / radius / spacing come from `src/shared/styles/tokens.css` via Tailwind / UnoCSS utilities. Never hard-code `#3b82f6` or `border-radius: 8px` inline.

### R8 — No hidden global state

State lives in:
- a component (local `useState`/`ref`),
- the URL (search params / route params),
- a server cache (`%DATA_LIB%`), or
- a named module store (`src/stores/<name>.store.ts`).

Module-scoped `let` variables are forbidden.

### R9 — Tests next to the code

`*.test.ts` colocated with the source. Use `%TESTS_LIB%`. New shared logic in `src/shared/` needs at least one test. UI changes need at least one realistic example/story when the stack supports it.

### R10 — Stop on uncertainty

If a requirement is ambiguous (auth shape, backend origin, error semantics, design intent), **ask before coding**. Silent assumptions are the most expensive bugs in this codebase.

## Naming

- Files: kebab-case (`user-card.tsx`), except framework-required (`+layout.tsx`, `App.vue`, `_app.tsx`).
- Components: PascalCase.
- Hooks / composables: `useXxx`.
- Stores: `useXxxStore`.
- Services: `xxx.service.ts`.
- API clients: `xxx.api.ts`.
- Types: `xxx.types.ts`.
- Schemas: `xxx.schema.ts`.
- Tests: `xxx.test.ts` / `xxx.spec.ts`.

## Quality bar before reporting done

- `%PACKAGE_MANAGER% typecheck` passes (or stack equivalent).
- `%PACKAGE_MANAGER% lint` passes.
- `%PACKAGE_MANAGER% test` passes (when tests touched).
- New UI rendered and visually inspected — type-check ≠ feature works.
- All four async states implemented when the change touches async data.
- No hand-rolled re-implementation of anything `%UI_LIBRARY%` already provides (see R1).

## Output format

After any non-trivial change, the agent's final message MUST end with:

```
Files changed: <list>
Checks run: <commands + pass/fail>
Known gaps: <list or "none">
```

## When the user provides a reference image / design

1. Identify which `%UI_LIBRARY%` primitives the image is built from. Name them explicitly before coding.
2. Map any custom-looking element to the closest library primitive + token-driven styling.
3. Only fall back to bespoke markup for genuinely novel layout (the structural skeleton between cards), never for the cards/buttons/inputs themselves.
4. State your component-mapping plan in chat first, then implement.

If `%UI_LIBRARY%` truly has no analog for a piece of the design, say so explicitly and propose either (a) compose a new component out of headless primitives from the same library, or (b) defer the bespoke piece behind a flag and ship the rest. Never ad-hoc invent.
